Day 3 – Part 1: What is a Linked List?
Basic definition (remember this)

A Linked List is a collection of nodes where each node stores data and the address of the next node.

What is a Node?

A node has 2 parts:

Data – the value (5, 10, 15)

Next – points to the next node

The last node points to NULL, meaning the list ends there.

Real-life analogy

Think of a treasure hunt.

Paper 1 says: "Go to Paper 2."

Paper 2 says: "Go to Paper 3."

Paper 3 says: "Stop."

Each paper knows only the next location, just like each node knows only the next node.

Behind the scenes

Unlike an array, Linked List nodes do not need to sit next to each other in memory.

The OS stores each node wherever free memory is available, and the next pointer connects them.

Why do companies use it?

Fast insertion at the beginning.

Fast deletion when you already have the node.

Used inside HashMaps, LRU Cache, Browser History, and OS schedulers.




Day 3 – Part 2: Head and Traversal
Basic definition

The head is the first node of a Linked List. Without the head, we cannot access the list.

The head points to the first node (10). From there, we follow next pointers to visit every node.

Behind the scenes

The computer does this:

Head
 ↓
10 → 20 → 30 → NULL

Start at Head.

Read 10.

Follow next to 20.

Follow next to 30.

Stop at NULL.

This is called traversal.

Python example
current = head

while current:
    print(current.data)
    current = current.next

Don't memorize it—we'll practice writing it later.

