Day 3 – Part 3: Insert at the Beginning (Head)
Basic definition (easy to remember)

Insert at the beginning means creating a new node and making it the new head.

Before
We want to insert 2

Result:

Head → 2 → 5 → 8 → 12 → NULL
Behind the scenes (most important)

The computer does 2 steps:

Step 1: New node points to the old head.

2 → 5

Step 2: Move the head to the new node.

Head
 ↓
2 → 5 → 8 → 12

Notice that we never move all the nodes. We only change two pointers, which is why this operation is very fast.

Python code
new_node.next = head
head = new_node

Don't memorize it—remember the 2-step idea.

Why is it O(1)?

Because no matter if there are 5 nodes or 5 million nodes, we only change two pointers.

